          "source_text_sha256": "a44044638e882e28bd6c72a1ca016e23a295062e16fae291237c5ff7b38b6993",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On the night of April 2, 2004, several witnesses observed a very bright light to the West among the stars. At the scene, gendarmes determined that the light was at approximately 30° to the horizon and appeared to be moving very slowly. A gendarmerie investigation will show that the observed point was Venus seen among a cluster of stars, the Pleiades, often mistaken for the Little Bear. This phenomenon is rare and occurs every eight years. The case is classified A: Venus observations.",
